Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.50,0:00:05.40,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}This is All in a Row: Compressed Convolution Networks for Graphs,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:05.48,0:00:09.70,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}an ICML 2023 paper from the Chinese Academy of Sciences.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:09.78,0:00:15.22,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Can we run standard, CNN-style Euclidean convolution directly on graphs?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:15.30,0:00:20.28,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Yes: learn a differentiable permutation that lines nodes in a row,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:20.36,0:00:22.73,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}then slide a kernel across them.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:22.81,0:00:25.72,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The model, CoCN, sets state of the art.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:26.10,0:00:29.32,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Convolutional graph networks have two weaknesses.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:29.40,0:00:32.69,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Because their filters come from graph polynomials,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:32.77,0:00:36.26,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}they lack expressiveness on a small parameter budget.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:36.34,0:00:40.50,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}And they cannot learn hierarchical, multi-scale features alone,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:40.58,0:00:44.27,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}needing bolt-on tricks like clustering or node dropping.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:44.35,0:00:51.41,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Image CNNs have neither problem: local learning and shared filters naturally capture multi-scale patterns.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:51.49,0:00:55.12,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}But their Euclidean convolution assumes a regular grid,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:55.20,0:00:57.41,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and does not fit irregular graphs.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:57.79,0:01:01.10,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}So why not borrow Euclidean convolution for graphs?
Dialogue: 0,0:01:01.18,0:01:03.89,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Convolution is sensitive to spatial order,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:03.97,0:01:07.15,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}but graph networks must be permutation invariant.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:07.23,0:01:11.93,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Earlier methods picked a node ordering independently of the convolution,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:12.01,0:01:16.25,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}so it could not be tuned for the task and often lost information.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:16.33,0:01:21.37,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}What is missing is an ordering that is differentiable and learned end to end.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:21.75,0:01:25.25,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The paper makes two contributions.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:25.33,0:01:28.82,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}First,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:28.90,0:01:32.40,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}a differentiable regularization for graphs: a learnable permutation that lines nodes into
Dialogue: 0,0:01:32.48,0:01:35.98,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}a single row while preserving permutation invariance,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:36.06,0:01:39.55,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}so Euclidean convolution finally applies.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:39.63,0:01:43.13,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Second, the model built on it, the Compressed Convolution Network, or CoCN.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:43.21,0:01:46.70,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}It learns both node and structure features and trains end to end,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:46.78,0:01:50.28,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}beating competitive GNNs and pooling models.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:50.66,0:01:54.04,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Here is how CoCN works.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:54.12,0:01:57.50,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}First,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:57.58,0:02:00.96,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}permutation generation regresses an absolute position for each node with an MLP on Laplacian-smoothed features,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:01.04,0:02:04.43,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}so similar nodes land nearby.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:04.51,0:02:07.89,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}These form a relaxed permutation matrix,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:07.97,0:02:11.35,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}controlled by a factor tau that provably converges to a true permutation.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:11.43,0:02:14.81,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}With nodes in a row,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:14.89,0:02:18.27,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}CoCN slides a kernel along the diagonal of the permuted edge and node matrices,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:18.35,0:02:21.73,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}extracting node features and their structure together.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:21.81,0:02:25.20,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Compressed pooling builds a hierarchy,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:25.28,0:02:28.66,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and transposed convolution up-samples for predictions.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:28.74,0:02:32.12,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Crucially, this cuts permutation modeling from n factorial to n squared,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:32.20,0:02:35.58,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}letting it scale to large graphs.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:35.96,0:02:37.74,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The evaluation is broad.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:37.82,0:02:44.19,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}For graph classification, six datasets: MUTAG, PROTEINS, NCI1, COLLAB, IMDB-Binary,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:44.27,0:02:47.91,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and IMDB-Multi, under ten-fold cross-validation.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:47.99,0:02:54.36,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}For node classification, six heterophilic benchmarks: Chameleon, Squirrel, Cornell,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:54.44,0:02:56.53,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Texas, Wisconsin, and Actor.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:56.61,0:03:02.51,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}To rule out leakage they test filtered Chameleon and Squirrel, and for scale,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:02.59,0:03:08.33,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}mini-batch experiments on three graphs with hundreds of thousands of nodes.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:08.71,0:03:11.17,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Results are strong across the board.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:11.25,0:03:16.75,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}On node classification, CoCN gets the best average rank, one point eight three,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:16.83,0:03:21.62,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}topping Chameleon at seventy-nine percent, Squirrel at seventy-three,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:21.70,0:03:23.45,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and Cornell at eighty-six.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:23.53,0:03:27.26,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}On graph classification, it wins five of six datasets,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:27.34,0:03:29.52,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}including COLLAB and IMDB-Multi.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:29.60,0:03:34.04,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}And on filtered Chameleon and Squirrel, with duplicates removed,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:34.12,0:03:38.77,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}it ranks first of eighteen models, so its edge is not data leakage.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:39.15,0:03:42.68,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Several ablations show why CoCN works.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:42.76,0:03:46.28,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}First, permutation heads matter: with more permutations,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:46.36,0:03:49.89,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}accuracy rises consistently on MUTAG, Chameleon, Cornell, and Wisconsin,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:49.97,0:03:53.49,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}since each exposes different node arrangements.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:53.57,0:03:57.10,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Second, larger diagonal kernels give a bigger receptive field and better performance,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:57.18,0:04:00.70,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}without over-smoothing.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:00.78,0:04:04.31,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Third,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:04.39,0:04:07.91,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}an information-loss study finds the relaxed permutations preserve almost all information,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:07.99,0:04:11.52,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}so the regularization is lossless.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:11.90,0:04:17.39,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}CoCN reaches an average rank of one point eight three across six node datasets,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:17.47,0:04:19.02,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}the best of any method.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:19.10,0:04:22.62,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}On Squirrel it scores nearly seventy-three percent,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:22.70,0:04:26.22,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}over eleven points above the strongest prior model.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:26.30,0:04:28.83,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}On COLLAB it hits eighty-six percent.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:28.91,0:04:34.12,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}And it cuts permutation modeling from intractable n factorial to n squared.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:34.50,0:04:36.11,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The takeaway is elegant.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:36.19,0:04:41.96,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Learn a differentiable permutation that arranges a graph's nodes into a single row,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:42.04,0:04:47.03,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and all the machinery of image convolution becomes available for graphs.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:47.11,0:04:51.11,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}CoCN does exactly this, provably convergent and invariant,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:51.19,0:04:53.51,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}reaching state-of-the-art results.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:53.59,0:04:57.10,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Graphs, all in a row, can be convolved like images.
